<!doctype html><html lang="en-CA"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><title>Index | PictureThis Production</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap"/></head><body><a href="#main">Skip to content</a><header><a href="/"><b>PICTURETHIS PRODUCTION</b></a><nav><a href="/">Home</a><a href="/programs/">Programs</a><a href="/faithfire/">Faithfire</a><a href="/projects/">Projects</a><a href="/blog/">Blog</a><a href="/events/">Events</a><a href="/partners/">Partners</a><a href="/get-involved/">Get Involved</a><a href="/donate/">Donate</a></nav></header><main id="main"><section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Our vision · Free training and mentoring / Ages 16–29</p><div class="hero-grid"><div><h1>A Toronto where young people build their future together, not on the streets, but as a crew: learning skills, earning a living, and looking out for one another.</h1><div class="lead"><h2>Our mission</h2><p>PictureThis Production gives young people ages 16 to 29 free, hands-on training and mentorship in live production, in sound, cameras, lighting, and streaming, on real shows alongside working technicians. They leave with a trade, a reference, and a path into paid work.</p></div><a class="button" href="/get-involved/">Get Involved</a><a href="/programs/">Programs</a></div><figure><img src="https://doop.design/photos/pexels/28643185.jpeg?h=650&w=940" alt="Audio engineer at a console"/><figcaption>Illustrative photography</figcaption></figure></div></section><div class="strip"><span>January</span><span>Saturdays 10:00 AM–2:00 PM</span><span>Free · Twenty places</span></div><section class="split"><p class="eyebrow">01 / WHY WE EXIST</p><div><h2>Our story</h2><p>PictureThis Production started with working technicians who kept meeting the same person: a young person with talent and no way in. No gear, no connections, no one to show them how a show actually runs. So we opened the room. PictureThis is the nonprofit that handles the training and mentoring. Faithfire Production, our production arm, is where the learning happens: real services, real events, real audiences, with participants working alongside the technicians who do this every week.</p><p>The first cohort starts in January: twenty places, eight weeks, free. Participants shadow, then assist, then run positions on real productions, paired with a mentor the whole way. Graduates go where the work is: church production teams, corporate AV crews, concert and event calls across the GTA.</p><h2>What we believe</h2><p></p><ul class="values"><li><h2>The craft is real.</h2><p>Training happens on working gear, at real shows, to a professional standard. Nothing is pretend.</p></li><li><h2>Nobody is behind.</h2><p>Everyone starts at week 1 together. Questions are part of the job, at every level.</p></li><li><h2>Conduct is part of the craft.</h2><p>How you show up, on comms and off, is taught, practised and expected.</p></li><li><h2>Care is a system, not a hope.</h2><p>Checked mentors, a written code of conduct, and meetings in crew spaces. Safety is designed, not assumed.</p></li><li><h2>No promises we cannot keep.</h2><p>We promise hours on real gear, honest teaching and a reference. The rest is earned, together.</p></li><li><h2>Free means free.</h2><p>Participants pay nothing, ever. Partners and donors carry the cost, and we say plainly where money goes.</p></li></ul></div></section><div class="photo-band"><figure><img src="https://doop.design/photos/pexels/28643185.jpeg?h=650&w=940" alt="Audio engineer at a console"/><figcaption>01 / Behind the switcher · Illustrative photography</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://doop.design/photos/pexels/28643185.jpeg?h=650&w=940" alt="Audio engineer at a console"/><figcaption>02 / Behind the camera · Illustrative photography</figcaption></figure></div><section class="soft"><p class="eyebrow">02 / PROGRAMS</p><div class="numbered"><div><h2>Training</h2><p>An eight-week course, Saturdays 10:00 AM to 2:00 PM, at Faithfire Production, 139 Millwick Dr, and partner venues across the GTA. Cables and signal flow in week 1; a full production crewed by the cohort in week 8.</p></div><div><h2>Mentoring</h2><p>Every participant is paired with a working technician: someone who mixed, switched or lit a show this month. Monthly check-ins at minimum, help with first bookings, rates, and when to say no.</p></div><div><h2>Pathways</h2><p>First bookings are usually cable and stagehand calls, then operating positions. We don't promise you a job. We promise hands-on time with real gear, honest instruction, a reference from people the industry knows, and a crew that'll say your name when a call comes in.</p></div><div><h2>Production Management</h2><p>The other half of putting on a show: advancing the event, building the run sheet and call sheets, managing the budget, and calling the show on the day. Participants who work well on a crew learn to run the production, not just handle one role in it.</p></div><div><h2>Technical Management</h2><p>For those who go on to lead a department: planning the rig, choosing and maintaining the gear, signing off on safety, and teaching the next cohort. It's the top of the pathway, the people who spec out the room and are accountable for it.</p></div></div><a class="button" href="/programs/">Programs</a></section><section class="crew split"><p class="eyebrow">03 / A PLACE ON THE CREW</p><div><h2>Pathways</h2><p>First bookings are usually cable and stagehand calls, then operating positions. We don't promise you a job. We promise hands-on time with real gear, honest instruction, a reference from people the industry knows, and a crew that'll say your name when a call comes in.</p><h2>Our standards</h2><p><ul><li>Every mentor passes a Vulnerable Sector Check before working with participants and follows a written code of conduct.</li><li>Mentors and participants meet in crew spaces, never alone off-site.</li><li>Participants aged 16 and 17 join with a parent or guardian's consent, and a parent or guardian can reach the program lead at any time.</li><li>Photos and recordings of participants are used only with consent, and consent can be withdrawn.</li><li>The program is free, and it stays free. No fees, no gear purchases, no paid upsells.</li><li>Anyone can raise a concern, about safety, conduct or anything else, and it will be heard by a named person, not a form. (Contact route to be confirmed by Rey.)</li></ul></p><blockquote><p>"Working on the production team has been one of the most rewarding things I've done. It gave me room to grow in ways I never expected. From the day I joined, I got to build my skills step by step, and in time I grew into the Technical Manager role, where I could put what I'd learned to work, contribute to the team, and help it succeed.</p><p>As the year ends, I've been promoted to Crew Chief, and I'm excited and grateful. It's another step in my growth, and I'm looking forward to the responsibility: to keep learning, to lead well, and to support this team the way it supported me."</p></blockquote><p>— Veronica Alexander, Crew Chief at FaithFire Production</p><h2>Cohort One — the Faithfire story</h2><p>The first cohort wasn't a class. It was a crew. Faithfire Production is where PictureThis got its start: a church production team at 139 Millwick Dr that grew into a full production department, one show at a time. Most of the crew started with little to no experience. They learned the way this program teaches today: by shadowing someone more experienced, helping out on real shows, and then running their own positions.</p><p>These days, that crew handles two Sunday services plus events like Cue & A and Revival Hub, and other churches and production teams specifically request them. They're also the go-to mentors for everyone who comes after them: the folks teaching cables in week 1 are the same ones who learned it all in that very room.</p><p>That's the integration, plain and simple: PictureThis is the nonprofit behind the mission, the training, and the standards. Faithfire is its production arm, and its first success story. Every new cohort learns inside a real, working production company, not a simulation.</p><a class="button" href="/faithfire/">Faithfire</a></div></section><section class="involved"><p class="eyebrow">04 / GET INVOLVED</p><div class="numbered"><div><h2>Join a cohort</h2><p>Ages 16 to 29, no experience needed. Applications open for January.</p></div><div><h2>Mentor</h2><p>Working technicians: one participant, monthly check-ins, real shows together. Screening and a code of conduct apply, and we will walk you through both.</p></div><div><h2>Partner or donate</h2><p>Venues, schools, churches and companies: host a training day, lend gear, fund a cohort, or hire our graduates for your calls.</p></div></div><a class="button" href="/get-involved/">Get Involved</a><a href="/donate/">Donate</a></section><section class="poster"><p class="eyebrow">05 / YOUR NEXT STEP</p><h2>January</h2><div class="strip"><span>Saturdays 10:00 AM–2:00 PM</span><span>Free · Twenty places</span></div><a class="button" href="/get-involved/">Get Involved</a></section></main><footer><p>Training: Faithfire Production, 139 Millwick Dr, Toronto.</p><p>PictureThis Production is being set up as a nonprofit organization in Ontario.</p><p>Faithfire Production is its production arm.</p><a href="/policies/">Our standards & policies</a> · <a href={"mailto:"+contactEmail}>{contactEmail}</a> · Placeholder contact</footer></body></html>